WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.952
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.952 --> 00:00:03.904
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.904 --> 00:00:07.808
[CLICKING]

00:00:16.129 --> 00:00:19.070
PROFESSOR: Today,
we're moving on,

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:21.590
and we're moving
into what's really

00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:24.470
happening with those electrons.

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:31.820
And I framed this on Monday by
telling you that the Rutherford

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:35.270
atom wasn't going to
make it more than 10

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:40.962
to the minus 11 seconds-ish
because classical E&amp;M would

00:00:40.962 --> 00:00:43.420
tell you that the electron, if
it's going around like this,

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:47.200
is radiating energy,
and so it spirals down

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:50.210
into that positively
charged nucleus.

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:52.790
So Rutherford got it
right in the sense

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:59.270
that you got all this positive
charge concentrated in this one

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:00.800
tiny little volume
in the middle,

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:02.300
and the electrons
are way out there.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:03.890
That's the planetary model.

00:01:03.890 --> 00:01:06.460
But we needed some new
theory to understand

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:08.210
why the whole thing
was stable, and that's

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:11.930
where Bohr comes along,
and that's what we're

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:13.980
going to talk about today.

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:21.580
Now, we need to feel
our oneness with waves.

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:26.220
Now, I think probably most of
about waves, but since we're

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:28.390
going to be talking about
them a whole lot today

00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:30.515
and as we go forward, I
want to make sure we're all

00:01:30.515 --> 00:01:31.510
on the same page.

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:32.547
So here's a wave.

00:01:32.547 --> 00:01:33.880
Well, we know about water waves.

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:35.005
They move a meter a second.

00:01:35.005 --> 00:01:36.880
Sound waves a little
faster, right?

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:39.070
And you've got these
characteristics of a wave--

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:43.840
the frequency of the
wave, the wavelength.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:46.330
And so I just want to make
sure that we're all together

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:51.710
on this because we're
going to be talking

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:53.300
about these variables a lot.

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:58.640
So if you have a wavelength,
that might be in meters.

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:02.442
How many meters do
you have per wave?

00:02:02.442 --> 00:02:03.650
We're getting somewhere here.

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:06.830
If you have the frequency-- and
I'll use this notation here.

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:07.847
You can use f.

00:02:07.847 --> 00:02:08.930
You might see f sometimes.

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:09.920
I like using nu.

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:11.840
And if you have the
frequency, well, that's

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:18.140
going to be the number of
waves per second, for example.

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:24.090
And so the speed is going to
be the two of them multiplied

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:24.590
together.

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:26.215
And I know a lot of
you have seen this,

00:02:26.215 --> 00:02:28.110
but again, I want
to get it up here.

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:30.920
So it's going to be
meters per second

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:35.280
if these are the units that
I'm using, seconds and meters.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.290
Now, if you're light--

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:39.970
light as in light--

00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:43.400
then you have a speed that
is a constant in vacuum,

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:44.890
and we know that.

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:46.930
So the speed of light--

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:49.710
so for light, let's put
that right underneath.

00:02:49.710 --> 00:03:01.290
For light, the speed is
c, which equals 3 times 10

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:03.600
to the eighth meters per second.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:05.380
OK, just getting
to some good stuff.

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:10.320
So if I wanted to
know, for example, how

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:12.360
to go back and forth
between frequency

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:15.330
and wavelength
for light, well, I

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:18.000
can just use this relationship.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:19.530
So let's put an
example up there.

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:33.160
So for example, if you're red
light, so if you're red light,

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:38.920
then you might have a frequency
of around, let's say, 450.

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:45.420
There's a range, but 450
terahertz would be a frequency.

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:47.100
And so now I can
get the wavelength

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:48.640
because I have
that relationship.

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:50.400
So the wavelength
for red light is

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:58.650
c over nu, which is going
to be equal to 3 times 10

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:07.500
to the eighth meters per
second divided by 450 times 10

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:09.990
to the 12th per second, right?

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:13.590
That's the measurement of
hertz is 1 over seconds, right?

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:15.440
That's frequency,
1 over seconds.

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:21.670
That gives me a number that
seems like it's pretty small,

00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:30.300
0.000000667 meters.

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:32.460
And so we could use
a different unit

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:36.840
like nanometers, which is
just 10 to the minus ninth.

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:42.450
So we could call that the
wavelength of red light, 667

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:42.990
nanometers.

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:46.027
Now, I'm doing this all slowly
and carefully because we're

00:04:46.027 --> 00:04:47.610
going to be doing
this a lot, and so I

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:50.220
want to make sure that
you're comfortable with this.

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:54.540
And we're going to add something
to this in just a minute.

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:56.628
If I was blue light--

00:04:56.628 --> 00:04:57.420
where's blue light?

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:58.337
Let's put blue light--

00:04:58.337 --> 00:05:00.600
I'm going to leave
red light here.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:04.680
And we'll put blue
light right above it

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:07.290
so that you can
see them together.

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:12.090
So blue might be
something like, oh,

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:17.310
a frequency of 650 terahertz,
so higher frequency.

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:20.340
And that would give you
a wavelength of something

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:23.923
like 460 nanometers.

00:05:23.923 --> 00:05:25.590
I'm putting these
both here because I'll

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:31.650
be comparing other aspects
of these colors later.

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:33.180
Now comes back-- no.

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:35.580
That's our oneness moment
with waves and light,

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:37.530
and now we come
back to our story,

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:41.640
our detective story of what's
happening inside of atoms,

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:43.840
and it really requires
us to go back.

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:49.620
I said Bohr had some
postulates about quantization.

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:53.760
There was work going on at
the time already related

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:54.810
to quantization.

00:05:57.470 --> 00:05:59.880
So I want to share
with you that work

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:02.850
because it's what inspired
Bohr, and then we'll

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:03.870
do the Bohr model.

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:06.420
But also just I want to get
you thinking about what this

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:07.980
all means.

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:09.840
So if you think about
music, you can think

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:11.098
about these two instruments.

00:06:11.098 --> 00:06:13.140
This might feel like--
the violin might feel like

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:15.750
something where you can
just play continuous notes,

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:18.670
whereas here you can't.

00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:20.410
It's discrete.

00:06:20.410 --> 00:06:23.380
I can't play any
notes in between.

00:06:23.380 --> 00:06:24.780
Something is discrete.

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:26.750
It's quantized.

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:28.460
Well, that's pretty obvious.

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:29.210
You look at those.

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:31.000
You're like, OK,
that makes sense.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:33.260
There's some things that
aren't as obvious, like here.

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:35.800
Oh, notice what's
in the calendar.

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:36.590
That's a screen.

00:06:36.590 --> 00:06:42.460
Now a screen from far enough
away might not look quantized,

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:47.470
but then when you look
at it closely, it is.

00:06:47.470 --> 00:06:48.710
It's pixels.

00:06:48.710 --> 00:06:50.860
There's nothing in between.

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:53.630
There's nothing in between.

00:06:53.630 --> 00:06:57.980
The thing is that at the time,
people thought about the world

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:00.500
in a certain way.

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:02.780
There's some things where
it's just obvious they're

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:07.328
violins like light.

00:07:07.328 --> 00:07:08.870
And then they're
like, wait a second.

00:07:08.870 --> 00:07:12.280
These things are
acting like xylophones.

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:14.980
What's going on?

00:07:14.980 --> 00:07:17.890
So Planck's contribution
was really--

00:07:17.890 --> 00:07:21.010
he was one of the first
kind of deep thinkers

00:07:21.010 --> 00:07:27.140
to talk about quantization, and
it was quantization of light.

00:07:27.140 --> 00:07:29.700
It was quantization of light.

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:33.340
And so what he said-- and this
is together with Einstein,

00:07:33.340 --> 00:07:35.340
and I'll show you what
Einstein did in a minute.

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:39.050
So it's called the
Planck-Einstein relation.

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:42.960
And what he said is that
the energy of a photon--

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:47.430
so Planck-- why did
I just go all caps?

00:07:47.430 --> 00:07:49.410
I don't know.

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:51.360
I don't know.

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:55.950
There's a reason,
Planck-Einstein.

00:07:55.950 --> 00:08:02.100
They said that the energy
for light-- the energy

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:08.400
is related to the frequency
through a constant.

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:16.470
And so in particular, E
equals h times the frequency.

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:18.820
Now, this was revolutionary.

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:20.980
It might just look like
a simple relationship.

00:08:20.980 --> 00:08:24.520
Oh, and by the way,
this is a constant

00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:28.650
named Planck's constant.

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:30.790
I'm going lowercase--

00:08:30.790 --> 00:08:32.049
Planck's constant.

00:08:32.049 --> 00:08:35.380
And, like other
constants, it has

00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:38.320
a value that doesn't change.

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:41.260
So that value for
Planck's constant is in--

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:43.010
I'll give it to you
in two different units

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:46.090
so we have it up here, 6.26--

00:08:46.090 --> 00:08:48.670
no-- 6.62-- sorry--

00:08:48.670 --> 00:08:55.990
6.626 times 10 to the
minus 34 joules-second.

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:05.130
Or it's also equal
to 4.136 times 10

00:09:05.130 --> 00:09:09.360
to the minus 15th
electronvolt-seconds.

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:14.310
Notice the units are in energy
times time, Planck's constant.

00:09:17.610 --> 00:09:20.790
And you can see that it must
be because this is energy

00:09:20.790 --> 00:09:24.210
and this is 1 over
time, the frequency.

00:09:24.210 --> 00:09:27.810
So it's got to have those
units for that to work.

00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:31.830
But this-- did I make a mistake?

00:09:31.830 --> 00:09:32.330
I'm worried.

00:09:32.330 --> 00:09:33.978
The dates are still
hitting me hard.

00:09:33.978 --> 00:09:36.020
I'm still thinking about
that, having nightmares.

00:09:36.020 --> 00:09:38.560
100 years off, 90 years off.

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:40.160
I'm good.

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:51.290
So what they saw is that
light had a frequency,

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:56.360
but it also had somehow
this unit of energy,

00:09:56.360 --> 00:10:01.970
a unit, a discrete
unit of energy

00:10:01.970 --> 00:10:04.715
that you can now calculate
using Planck's constant.

00:10:07.250 --> 00:10:08.540
And that was revolutionary.

00:10:08.540 --> 00:10:10.850
That was not what
people thought.

00:10:10.850 --> 00:10:14.550
And it really all came
home in what Einstein did,

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:17.210
which was the
experiments that he

00:10:17.210 --> 00:10:19.400
won the Nobel Prize
for-- his deeper thinking

00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:21.650
was in relativity, but he
won the Nobel Prize for this

00:10:21.650 --> 00:10:27.880
because it was absolutely
essential to further

00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:33.520
this idea of quantization and
show it with an experiment.

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:35.160
And because it was
so important, I

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:39.160
want to share that experiment
because when Einstein did

00:10:39.160 --> 00:10:40.870
was really quite amazing.

00:10:40.870 --> 00:10:45.310
He shined light on a
metal, and what he saw--

00:10:45.310 --> 00:10:49.210
so this is called the
photoelectric effect--

00:10:49.210 --> 00:10:54.290
photoelectric effect.

00:10:54.290 --> 00:11:01.840
And this is where you
take a piece of metal-

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:05.800
and they knew there were
electrons in there, right?

00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:07.397
That discovery had happened.

00:11:07.397 --> 00:11:08.980
So they knew there
were electrons in--

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:11.188
and they also knew if you
hit this metal with energy,

00:11:11.188 --> 00:11:12.970
the electrons could come off.

00:11:12.970 --> 00:11:17.410
I mean, after all, that's
what the cathode ray tube was.

00:11:17.410 --> 00:11:21.990
It was electrons coming
off of a piece of metal.

00:11:21.990 --> 00:11:24.250
And in that experiment,
you hook up a voltage,

00:11:24.250 --> 00:11:26.630
but here he shined light.

00:11:26.630 --> 00:11:31.780
And so if you take, for example,
different frequencies of light

00:11:31.780 --> 00:11:34.540
and you shine them on this
same piece of metal-- so let's

00:11:34.540 --> 00:11:41.050
go red and then let's go
green and then let's go blue.

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:50.890
Now, if you shine red light on
the metal, nothing happened.

00:11:50.890 --> 00:11:53.250
Nothing happened.

00:11:53.250 --> 00:11:55.107
But OK, we know if
you just crank up

00:11:55.107 --> 00:11:57.440
the intensity of the light,
something's got to come off.

00:11:57.440 --> 00:11:59.220
You're putting all this energy--

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:00.690
no.

00:12:00.690 --> 00:12:01.190
Why?

00:12:01.190 --> 00:12:06.260
Because the particles, the
packets, the quantized amounts

00:12:06.260 --> 00:12:09.170
of energy that that
kind of light can have

00:12:09.170 --> 00:12:12.330
is determined here.

00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:14.420
So what Einstein says
is, well, OK, then these

00:12:14.420 --> 00:12:17.090
must be particles.

00:12:17.090 --> 00:12:19.370
They must have some
energy associated

00:12:19.370 --> 00:12:23.990
with a particle that follows
this relationship equals h nu.

00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:26.600
And notice that doesn't
depend on intensity.

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:28.960
It just depends on frequency.

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:31.590
So I might be hitting this metal
with more and more of them,

00:12:31.590 --> 00:12:34.550
but each one only has
the same amount of energy

00:12:34.550 --> 00:12:37.350
limited by that relationship.

00:12:37.350 --> 00:12:40.690
So if I want to hit those
electrons with more energy,

00:12:40.690 --> 00:12:43.720
I've got to change
the frequency.

00:12:43.720 --> 00:12:45.740
And so what was found
in this experiment

00:12:45.740 --> 00:12:48.810
is that, well, maybe
here you get nothing.

00:12:48.810 --> 00:12:51.170
So here you get nothing.

00:12:51.170 --> 00:12:53.480
How am I going to write that?

00:12:53.480 --> 00:12:54.410
OK, nothing.

00:12:54.410 --> 00:12:55.970
I just won't write it.

00:12:55.970 --> 00:12:59.270
Here, maybe you get electrons.

00:12:59.270 --> 00:13:07.400
And here, maybe you
get faster electrons

00:13:07.400 --> 00:13:12.530
because these fundamental
particles of energy of light

00:13:12.530 --> 00:13:14.870
have different energies, right?

00:13:14.870 --> 00:13:17.360
So now we can calculate it.

00:13:17.360 --> 00:13:21.560
We can calculate the
energy of that red light,

00:13:21.560 --> 00:13:23.600
and we calculate the
energy of the blue light

00:13:23.600 --> 00:13:28.610
because I have
now E equals h nu.

00:13:28.610 --> 00:13:31.010
And if I plug it in,
which I won't do,

00:13:31.010 --> 00:13:34.490
you get something like
1.8 electronvolts.

00:13:34.490 --> 00:13:38.480
And if you do the
same thing for blue,

00:13:38.480 --> 00:13:40.190
from the
Planck-Einstein relation

00:13:40.190 --> 00:13:46.190
you get the energy
is 2.7 electronvolts.

00:13:46.190 --> 00:13:51.140
So what Einstein did in this
experiment which was so crucial

00:13:51.140 --> 00:13:54.170
is he measured.

00:13:54.170 --> 00:13:55.970
He changed the
frequency of light

00:13:55.970 --> 00:13:58.550
that you shine on
this metal, and what

00:13:58.550 --> 00:14:00.896
he saw is that there was
nothing, nothing, nothing,

00:14:00.896 --> 00:14:01.640
nothing.

00:14:01.640 --> 00:14:04.340
And then all of a sudden
you literally had,

00:14:04.340 --> 00:14:08.280
if you plot the
kinetic energy of the--

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:09.590
I'll write it out here--

00:14:09.590 --> 00:14:15.495
ejected electron-- so that's
what's being plotted there.

00:14:18.110 --> 00:14:19.825
You plot that versus the--

00:14:19.825 --> 00:14:21.200
I said it wasn't
going to use f--

00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:23.540
versus the frequency of the
light, nothing, nothing,

00:14:23.540 --> 00:14:24.040
nothing.

00:14:24.040 --> 00:14:26.600
And then all of a sudden
there's a linear relationship

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:28.670
between them.

00:14:28.670 --> 00:14:31.190
That explains it
because these things

00:14:31.190 --> 00:14:36.650
were made of these particles
that were called photons.

00:14:36.650 --> 00:14:38.045
So photons.

00:14:42.260 --> 00:14:45.950
Now, while this was
blowing their minds

00:14:45.950 --> 00:14:48.690
and winning prizes and
all that, Bohr came along.

00:14:48.690 --> 00:14:51.380
Now remember, this is
what I mean by dates.

00:14:51.380 --> 00:14:54.170
Apart from the
dates, we know now

00:14:54.170 --> 00:14:58.610
there was a lot of time between
those experiments, especially

00:14:58.610 --> 00:15:01.150
Dalton and Thompson.

00:15:01.150 --> 00:15:05.480
And that's where
we were on Monday.

00:15:05.480 --> 00:15:08.060
So Bohr was looking at all this.

00:15:08.060 --> 00:15:09.620
Remember, he wrote
the paper where

00:15:09.620 --> 00:15:15.200
he said it is with great
interest the Rutherford atom,

00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:16.910
meaning it's wrong.

00:15:16.910 --> 00:15:17.942
It needs fixing.

00:15:17.942 --> 00:15:20.150
Meanwhile, Bohr's looking
over at Einstein and Planck

00:15:20.150 --> 00:15:21.440
and he's like, wait a second.

00:15:21.440 --> 00:15:24.490
Let me get some of
that quantization stuff

00:15:24.490 --> 00:15:28.420
because if it works for
light, what if it also

00:15:28.420 --> 00:15:30.190
works for electrons?

00:15:30.190 --> 00:15:32.680
What if it also
works for electrons?

00:15:32.680 --> 00:15:35.470
And so he's looking
at this stuff going on

00:15:35.470 --> 00:15:38.740
with Planck and Einstein, and
he applied it to the electron

00:15:38.740 --> 00:15:40.570
in Rutherford's atom.

00:15:40.570 --> 00:15:41.900
That's what he did.

00:15:41.900 --> 00:15:43.450
That's what Bohr did.

00:15:43.450 --> 00:15:46.000
He said, you guys
got your light thing.

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:48.750
I'm going after the atom.

00:15:48.750 --> 00:15:51.430
And what I'm going to do is
I'm going to make just actually

00:15:51.430 --> 00:15:54.880
a fairly simple postulate.

00:15:54.880 --> 00:15:56.630
Why is that up there?

00:15:56.630 --> 00:15:58.110
Let's go.

00:15:58.110 --> 00:16:02.820
I'm going to say that for
me, the quantization is

00:16:02.820 --> 00:16:05.110
going to happen with
the angular momentum.

00:16:05.110 --> 00:16:10.530
So Bohr came along, and
he said that the angular

00:16:10.530 --> 00:16:14.640
momentum of the electron--
so L equals mvr, right?

00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:17.640
Remember, we listed the Bohr
postulates, and one of them

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:22.300
was that Newtonian
mechanics would hold.

00:16:22.300 --> 00:16:24.610
Angular momentum, mvr.

00:16:24.610 --> 00:16:27.070
But there's something
added to it.

00:16:27.070 --> 00:16:29.770
He said that that angular
momentum could only have

00:16:29.770 --> 00:16:33.940
discrete values, quantization.

00:16:33.940 --> 00:16:37.510
So he said that this is
quantized, and he was specific.

00:16:40.720 --> 00:16:44.950
He said it could only be
some number, some integer

00:16:44.950 --> 00:16:48.400
times Planck's constant
divided by 2 pi.

00:16:48.400 --> 00:16:49.000
Why not?

00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:50.470
When in doubt, divide by 2 pi.

00:16:54.700 --> 00:16:59.140
As you all know, that has to do
with just making math easier.

00:16:59.140 --> 00:17:01.130
Now, that's the
angular momentum,

00:17:01.130 --> 00:17:02.380
and he said it's quantization.

00:17:02.380 --> 00:17:07.240
And n-- n is the key here
because n is this thing that

00:17:07.240 --> 00:17:12.250
says that it must be some unit.

00:17:12.250 --> 00:17:13.540
This is like the unit.

00:17:13.540 --> 00:17:14.859
It's like that photon energy.

00:17:14.859 --> 00:17:20.089
It comes in units, but it
comes in units of integers.

00:17:20.089 --> 00:17:26.920
So you could have 1 or you
could have 2 or 3 but not 1.5.

00:17:26.920 --> 00:17:28.480
That's quantization.

00:17:28.480 --> 00:17:31.510
So n had to be 1, 2, 3.

00:17:31.510 --> 00:17:33.730
It had to be some integer.

00:17:33.730 --> 00:17:37.150
And oh, here we go.

00:17:37.150 --> 00:17:37.883
Watch this.

00:17:37.883 --> 00:17:39.550
We're going to call
it a quantum number.

00:17:43.090 --> 00:17:45.986
I should have written
that in all caps.

00:17:45.986 --> 00:17:47.620
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:47.620 --> 00:17:50.860
Thank you for that.

00:17:50.860 --> 00:17:53.560
It's our first quantum
number, and we've only

00:17:53.560 --> 00:17:56.320
known each other for a week--

00:17:56.320 --> 00:17:57.190
a week.

00:17:57.190 --> 00:17:58.240
Here we are.

00:17:58.240 --> 00:18:00.890
We're quantization things.

00:18:00.890 --> 00:18:06.230
Quantum number because it's a
number that counts quantumness,

00:18:06.230 --> 00:18:08.090
quantization.

00:18:08.090 --> 00:18:10.910
This is how you get the
quantization from this.

00:18:10.910 --> 00:18:12.510
So it's a quantum number.

00:18:12.510 --> 00:18:14.330
Now here's the thing.

00:18:14.330 --> 00:18:17.570
I will not derive
Bohr's model for you.

00:18:17.570 --> 00:18:20.700
I will tell you what it is.

00:18:20.700 --> 00:18:25.580
And if you want, you can
look up the derivation.

00:18:25.580 --> 00:18:28.850
It's actually really
fairly straightforward.

00:18:28.850 --> 00:18:32.690
It basically has to do
with this one assumption,

00:18:32.690 --> 00:18:35.750
quantization of the
angular momentum

00:18:35.750 --> 00:18:39.320
of that electron,
combined with F equals ma

00:18:39.320 --> 00:18:41.450
and the electron is stable.

00:18:41.450 --> 00:18:43.260
It's not accelerating in.

00:18:43.260 --> 00:18:45.690
And when you do
that, what you get--

00:18:45.690 --> 00:18:49.400
let's put this-- no.

00:18:49.400 --> 00:18:51.390
Let's put that back up.

00:18:51.390 --> 00:18:57.110
So what you get with Bohr's
model is the following.

00:18:59.890 --> 00:19:06.320
You get three extremely
important outcomes.

00:19:06.320 --> 00:19:13.120
First, you get that the radius
of that electron is quantized.

00:19:17.290 --> 00:19:22.390
Then you get that it's energy.

00:19:22.390 --> 00:19:27.940
The energy that it's allowed
to have is also quantized.

00:19:27.940 --> 00:19:35.530
And finally, you get that
its transitions are--

00:19:38.190 --> 00:19:38.830
you got it.

00:19:38.830 --> 00:19:40.920
Thank you-- quantized.

00:19:43.590 --> 00:19:47.220
So if you plug stuff in and
you do the Bohr derivation

00:19:47.220 --> 00:19:49.530
for the hydrogen
atom, what you get

00:19:49.530 --> 00:19:53.370
is that the radius
of that electron

00:19:53.370 --> 00:19:58.020
is equal to n squared
times a constant divided

00:19:58.020 --> 00:20:00.000
by the atomic number.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:05.010
And this constant is
called the Bohr radius.

00:20:05.010 --> 00:20:13.170
It's got a value of
0.0529 nanometers,

00:20:13.170 --> 00:20:17.120
and it's also called the
Bohr radius if you want.

00:20:17.120 --> 00:20:18.780
But that's its value.

00:20:18.780 --> 00:20:22.890
And then if do the same--
now if you look at energy--

00:20:22.890 --> 00:20:28.040
so energy, the equation for
that comes out to be minus 13.6

00:20:28.040 --> 00:20:31.460
times the atomic
number squared divided

00:20:31.460 --> 00:20:36.890
by n squared in units of eV.

00:20:36.890 --> 00:20:39.530
Those are the two
key results that

00:20:39.530 --> 00:20:44.360
come from Bohr's postulates
and from Bohr doing this--

00:20:44.360 --> 00:20:44.930
doing that.

00:20:47.710 --> 00:20:49.850
And we're going to talk
about each one of these.

00:20:49.850 --> 00:20:51.100
All right, so if I--

00:20:51.100 --> 00:20:51.600
OK.

00:20:56.960 --> 00:20:59.370
So let's go one at a time.

00:20:59.370 --> 00:20:59.870
No.

00:21:03.107 --> 00:21:04.940
So let's talk about the
radius for a minute.

00:21:08.783 --> 00:21:09.950
Let's talk about the radius.

00:21:09.950 --> 00:21:14.160
So if I look at the radius
and I'm a nucleus here--

00:21:14.160 --> 00:21:16.040
so I've got like
some positive charge

00:21:16.040 --> 00:21:19.790
in the middle and some nucleons
and stuff, what I'm saying--

00:21:19.790 --> 00:21:22.550
I'm sorry, some
nuclei neutrons--

00:21:22.550 --> 00:21:29.825
is that the electron can
only be at certain values.

00:21:32.580 --> 00:21:38.660
This distance r can
only be at these values,

00:21:38.660 --> 00:21:42.560
and that's dictated
by the quantization

00:21:42.560 --> 00:21:43.910
number, the quantum number.

00:21:43.910 --> 00:21:45.988
n equals 1 would be the closest.

00:21:45.988 --> 00:21:47.405
You can see that
from the formula.

00:21:49.980 --> 00:21:55.420
And n equals 2, and so on.

00:21:55.420 --> 00:21:59.470
And this is not
necessarily drawn to scale.

00:21:59.470 --> 00:22:04.420
You can see that for n
equals 1 and z equals 1,

00:22:04.420 --> 00:22:09.220
the electron is simply at
the distance of the constant,

00:22:09.220 --> 00:22:15.700
0.5-ish angstroms,
0.05 nanometers, right?

00:22:15.700 --> 00:22:18.580
At n equals 2,
it's 4 times that.

00:22:18.580 --> 00:22:19.270
Oh, yeah?

00:22:19.270 --> 00:22:20.537
STUDENT: What's the z?

00:22:20.537 --> 00:22:22.120
PROFESSOR: The z is
the atomic number.

00:22:25.010 --> 00:22:27.680
And that comes in--

00:22:27.680 --> 00:22:28.520
let's put that here.

00:22:34.010 --> 00:22:36.140
And that comes in
because it tells us

00:22:36.140 --> 00:22:38.390
how many positive
charges there are,

00:22:38.390 --> 00:22:42.740
how many protons there
are in the nucleus.

00:22:42.740 --> 00:22:44.900
And that comes in when
you do F equals ma.

00:22:44.900 --> 00:22:47.300
That's how it comes
into the Bohr equations.

00:22:51.170 --> 00:22:53.570
And r is a distance, and
n is the quantum number,

00:22:53.570 --> 00:22:54.860
and a0 is a constant.

00:22:54.860 --> 00:22:56.600
z is the atomic
number in both cases.

00:23:02.330 --> 00:23:07.340
This is mind-blowing
because it tells you

00:23:07.340 --> 00:23:11.090
that that's where the electrons
are in the atom orbit,

00:23:11.090 --> 00:23:14.040
in the atom, but they're
only those distances.

00:23:14.040 --> 00:23:16.940
Remember, quantization
means nothing else.

00:23:16.940 --> 00:23:18.230
Think about that.

00:23:18.230 --> 00:23:20.450
I'm saying that that electron
can't be anywhere else.

00:23:20.450 --> 00:23:25.170
It must be this distance,
or it must be this distance.

00:23:25.170 --> 00:23:26.790
So this was a big deal.

00:23:26.790 --> 00:23:29.410
People were like, what?

00:23:29.410 --> 00:23:30.940
It didn't compute.

00:23:30.940 --> 00:23:31.940
It didn't make sense.

00:23:31.940 --> 00:23:33.850
But this is what
they were starting

00:23:33.850 --> 00:23:38.350
to see, that this was the only
way to explain what they saw,

00:23:38.350 --> 00:23:41.330
which we'll come to when
we talk about transitions.

00:23:41.330 --> 00:23:43.030
So this would be the radius.

00:23:43.030 --> 00:23:47.170
And when you look at the
energy, it's just as--

00:23:47.170 --> 00:23:48.690
I'm going to use--

00:23:48.690 --> 00:23:50.920
yeah, I'll put it here.

00:23:50.920 --> 00:23:53.650
So you take that
equation for the energy

00:23:53.650 --> 00:23:59.110
of an electron in an atom,
and you take z equals 1.

00:23:59.110 --> 00:24:04.980
If z equals 1, then the
energy is quantized,

00:24:04.980 --> 00:24:11.710
and it's minus 13.6 divided
by n squared electronvolts.

00:24:11.710 --> 00:24:17.645
And if I just draw this
as energy going up--

00:24:17.645 --> 00:24:19.020
so if I just draw
this, then what

00:24:19.020 --> 00:24:23.520
that means is that I've got
one line down here showing

00:24:23.520 --> 00:24:24.700
kind of the energy level.

00:24:24.700 --> 00:24:30.690
So this would be minus 13.6 eV.

00:24:30.690 --> 00:24:33.030
And then I've got
another line here,

00:24:33.030 --> 00:24:36.690
and this would be minus 3.4 eV.

00:24:36.690 --> 00:24:40.230
And then I've got
another one here, minus--

00:24:40.230 --> 00:24:43.400
let's see-- 1.5-ish eV.

00:24:43.400 --> 00:24:45.510
And I'm going to
start to get crowded.

00:24:45.510 --> 00:24:49.230
As you can see, they're going to
get closer and closer until you

00:24:49.230 --> 00:24:52.800
get to some level
here, which would

00:24:52.800 --> 00:24:54.652
be like n equals infinity.

00:24:58.430 --> 00:25:02.230
And then above that, zero.

00:25:02.230 --> 00:25:04.430
Above that is free.

00:25:04.430 --> 00:25:06.160
The electron is free.

00:25:06.160 --> 00:25:08.020
When its n is
infinity, when it's

00:25:08.020 --> 00:25:11.720
gone through all the quantum
numbers up to infinity,

00:25:11.720 --> 00:25:12.980
it's free.

00:25:12.980 --> 00:25:16.580
And that is called ionization,
taking it out of the atom

00:25:16.580 --> 00:25:19.460
and making it not part of
an atom anymore but just out

00:25:19.460 --> 00:25:22.520
there free able to
do what it wants.

00:25:22.520 --> 00:25:23.407
You've ionized it.

00:25:23.407 --> 00:25:24.240
You've taken it out.

00:25:24.240 --> 00:25:28.700
We'll come back to this
concept multiple times.

00:25:28.700 --> 00:25:31.280
These are the energy
levels for an electron,

00:25:31.280 --> 00:25:33.320
and they are also quantized.

00:25:33.320 --> 00:25:35.190
They could only be
this value or only

00:25:35.190 --> 00:25:36.440
that value or only that value.

00:25:36.440 --> 00:25:38.270
They cannot be anything
else in between.

00:25:38.270 --> 00:25:42.470
That was revolutionary
at the time.

00:25:42.470 --> 00:25:44.910
So this would be
like n equals 1.

00:25:44.910 --> 00:25:45.410
Sorry.

00:25:45.410 --> 00:25:47.750
That's not E. That's eV.

00:25:47.750 --> 00:25:53.040
n equals 1, n
equals 2, and so on.

00:25:53.040 --> 00:25:55.970
Now the third point that
I made is that it also

00:25:55.970 --> 00:25:57.620
tells us about transitions.

00:26:03.560 --> 00:26:06.380
This was also
absolutely essential,

00:26:06.380 --> 00:26:11.900
and this was the key piece to
understanding what they saw.

00:26:11.900 --> 00:26:14.780
And so we'll get to
that in a few minutes.

00:26:14.780 --> 00:26:16.130
What do I mean by a transition?

00:26:16.130 --> 00:26:20.510
I mean that an electron is
in an atom at some level,

00:26:20.510 --> 00:26:21.860
and it moves to another level.

00:26:25.010 --> 00:26:27.220
But now the point is
that if it can only

00:26:27.220 --> 00:26:29.230
be in these discrete
quantized levels,

00:26:29.230 --> 00:26:31.240
then when it moves
from one to another,

00:26:31.240 --> 00:26:35.320
those changes in energy
are also discrete values.

00:26:35.320 --> 00:26:37.930
There are only certain
changes in energy

00:26:37.930 --> 00:26:40.800
that that electron
can experience.

00:26:40.800 --> 00:26:43.110
And there's even
more which is that

00:26:43.110 --> 00:26:44.850
what can cause those
changes or what

00:26:44.850 --> 00:26:47.220
happens as a result
of those changes

00:26:47.220 --> 00:26:49.350
is the interaction with light.

00:26:49.350 --> 00:26:53.900
And we're all the way
back to here, photons.

00:26:53.900 --> 00:26:55.660
Think about it this way.

00:26:55.660 --> 00:26:58.150
If I take a ball and
I have it up here,

00:26:58.150 --> 00:26:59.980
it's got some mgh in it, right?

00:26:59.980 --> 00:27:01.950
And I drop it, and
now it's got some KE.

00:27:04.930 --> 00:27:07.930
More energy was in
gravitation, was

00:27:07.930 --> 00:27:10.745
stored in mgh, gravitational
potential energy,

00:27:10.745 --> 00:27:11.620
and then I let it go.

00:27:11.620 --> 00:27:15.450
Well, for an electron,
this is its ground state.

00:27:15.450 --> 00:27:18.190
This is the kind of happy place.

00:27:18.190 --> 00:27:22.470
So this would be
like a ground state,

00:27:22.470 --> 00:27:29.560
and these would be
all excited states.

00:27:29.560 --> 00:27:33.370
It would be like excited states.

00:27:33.370 --> 00:27:38.490
So that would be like lifting
the electron up and letting go.

00:27:38.490 --> 00:27:41.890
But when you let it go,
it's not kinetic energy

00:27:41.890 --> 00:27:43.500
that that transfers into.

00:27:43.500 --> 00:27:44.680
It's photon energy.

00:27:49.160 --> 00:27:54.500
Photon energy gets emitted from
electrons changing their levels

00:27:54.500 --> 00:27:55.850
in an atom.

00:27:55.850 --> 00:28:01.290
Light is an absolutely
intricate part of this system.

00:28:01.290 --> 00:28:03.660
And not only that, but
everything is quantized.

00:28:07.120 --> 00:28:09.730
I almost need a
moment, but I'm OK.

00:28:09.730 --> 00:28:10.990
I'm going to keep going.

00:28:10.990 --> 00:28:17.840
This was our big deal,
a really big deal

00:28:17.840 --> 00:28:20.720
because now I can tell you--

00:28:25.630 --> 00:28:27.910
OK, there we go--

00:28:27.910 --> 00:28:31.000
because now I could tell you,
for example, that if that's

00:28:31.000 --> 00:28:34.930
the second orbit in hydrogen
and that's the first orbit,

00:28:34.930 --> 00:28:36.730
I can tell you what
frequency light

00:28:36.730 --> 00:28:39.820
is emitted if you go
from n equals 2 to n

00:28:39.820 --> 00:28:47.590
equals 1 because the change
in energy from n equals 2 to n

00:28:47.590 --> 00:28:55.390
equals 1 is nothing more
than minus 13.6 eV times--

00:28:55.390 --> 00:28:58.450
well, it's the final state
minus the initial state, right?

00:28:58.450 --> 00:29:02.830
The final state is
1 over 1 squared,

00:29:02.830 --> 00:29:05.740
and the initial state
is 1 over 2 squared.

00:29:05.740 --> 00:29:11.290
All I've done is pull out the
13.6, but these are just--

00:29:11.290 --> 00:29:13.540
these are just the
Bohr-model energies,

00:29:13.540 --> 00:29:15.140
and I'm just taking
the difference.

00:29:15.140 --> 00:29:19.990
This is equal to Ef minus Ei.

00:29:19.990 --> 00:29:21.700
It's the change in energy.

00:29:21.700 --> 00:29:25.820
So in this case, it's
minus 10.2 electronvolts

00:29:25.820 --> 00:29:28.600
and going from the second
orbit, quantized orbit,

00:29:28.600 --> 00:29:30.250
down to the first.

00:29:30.250 --> 00:29:33.190
And I can now tell you because
of what we did in the beginning

00:29:33.190 --> 00:29:44.790
that that would emit a photon
of frequency 2.5 times 10

00:29:44.790 --> 00:29:46.500
to the 15th hertz.

00:29:49.280 --> 00:29:51.590
That's the energy
difference, and that energy

00:29:51.590 --> 00:29:53.868
goes into producing a photon--

00:29:53.868 --> 00:29:55.910
goes into producing a
photon, and we can tell you

00:29:55.910 --> 00:29:58.670
exactly what that frequency is.

00:29:58.670 --> 00:30:00.830
So these are the
kinds of transitions

00:30:00.830 --> 00:30:02.540
that the Bohr model
explains, and it

00:30:02.540 --> 00:30:06.090
explains energy changing
from electron transitions

00:30:06.090 --> 00:30:06.590
to photons.

00:30:09.290 --> 00:30:11.030
So what are the
implications of this?

00:30:11.030 --> 00:30:14.810
Oh, there's the question
that I just answered.

00:30:14.810 --> 00:30:17.120
If this is hydrogen, z equals 1.

00:30:21.220 --> 00:30:24.370
The implications of
this are groundbreaking,

00:30:24.370 --> 00:30:26.020
and it explained
what people were

00:30:26.020 --> 00:30:28.030
seeing because you
could be talking

00:30:28.030 --> 00:30:29.170
about angstroms already.

00:30:29.170 --> 00:30:31.630
Angstrom looked up, and he's
like, well, you guys think

00:30:31.630 --> 00:30:33.640
it's a rainbow, but
I'm not seeing that.

00:30:33.640 --> 00:30:36.728
I'm seeing discrete
lines all over the place.

00:30:36.728 --> 00:30:38.770
And that's what he saw
when he looked at the sun.

00:30:38.770 --> 00:30:40.060
Don't look at the sun.

00:30:40.060 --> 00:30:44.040
It's not a good idea,
but Angstrom did.

00:30:44.040 --> 00:30:46.830
And now he said, well, but
there are all these lines,

00:30:46.830 --> 00:30:50.740
and the wavelengths
that he tabulated

00:30:50.740 --> 00:30:52.810
happened to come on
this sort of thousands

00:30:52.810 --> 00:30:56.370
of angstrom kind of scale.

00:30:56.370 --> 00:30:58.770
And so he liked that,
and chemists love it,

00:30:58.770 --> 00:31:00.770
and that's why the angstrom
is named what it is,

00:31:00.770 --> 00:31:01.937
10 to the minus 10th meters.

00:31:05.260 --> 00:31:09.700
Chemists love it because
a bond is a few angstroms.

00:31:09.700 --> 00:31:11.710
Spectroscopists like nanometers.

00:31:11.710 --> 00:31:13.860
They're like,
thousands are too big.

00:31:13.860 --> 00:31:14.860
We don't like thousands.

00:31:14.860 --> 00:31:16.510
We want to work with hundreds.

00:31:16.510 --> 00:31:18.833
So spectroscopists
use nanometers.

00:31:18.833 --> 00:31:20.500
Other people, chemists,
whose angstroms.

00:31:20.500 --> 00:31:23.730
I say can't we all
just really get along?

00:31:23.730 --> 00:31:24.640
And it's fine.

00:31:24.640 --> 00:31:28.150
Don't make spectroscopists
angry, really.

00:31:28.150 --> 00:31:30.160
I did it once.

00:31:30.160 --> 00:31:35.370
Now on Friday, you
are going to get

00:31:35.370 --> 00:31:37.410
the most accurate
spectroscope that

00:31:37.410 --> 00:31:42.350
has been made by humans
in your goodie bag,

00:31:42.350 --> 00:31:44.540
and you'll be able
to look at the fact

00:31:44.540 --> 00:31:46.350
that the world is
not continuous.

00:31:46.350 --> 00:31:51.180
These frequencies, wavelengths
that are coming out

00:31:51.180 --> 00:31:52.830
are not continuous.

00:31:52.830 --> 00:31:57.210
If you take a tube of H2 gas and
you apply an enormous voltage

00:31:57.210 --> 00:32:04.260
to it, you will excite
electrons in the H2 molecules.

00:32:04.260 --> 00:32:06.660
As they go back down,
they will emit photons,

00:32:06.660 --> 00:32:07.620
and what will you see?

00:32:07.620 --> 00:32:09.300
Only certain values.

00:32:09.300 --> 00:32:11.390
Well, this is what
people were doing.

00:32:11.390 --> 00:32:13.890
They were looking out into the
universe-- which, by the way,

00:32:13.890 --> 00:32:21.760
is 75% hydrogen. And
they're like, really?

00:32:21.760 --> 00:32:23.419
The hydrogen economy.

00:32:23.419 --> 00:32:26.353
[LAUGHTER]

00:32:28.800 --> 00:32:34.810
We-- we-- they were looking
now and seeing discrete lines

00:32:34.810 --> 00:32:37.340
all over the place.

00:32:37.340 --> 00:32:38.800
How to explain it?

00:32:38.800 --> 00:32:41.560
The Bohr model is
what explained it.

00:32:41.560 --> 00:32:43.990
You will be able to see these
discrete lines yourselves

00:32:43.990 --> 00:32:45.790
with the goodie bag on Friday.

00:32:45.790 --> 00:32:51.760
But if you do this, this is what
you might see from hydrogen.

00:32:51.760 --> 00:32:53.620
And there were a
lot of people trying

00:32:53.620 --> 00:32:56.563
to understand this at the
time, Balmer and Rydberg.

00:32:56.563 --> 00:32:58.480
And I'm not going to go
through-- they came up

00:32:58.480 --> 00:33:00.130
with sort of partial formula.

00:33:00.130 --> 00:33:02.680
They were empirically based.

00:33:02.680 --> 00:33:05.500
But Bohr-- it was
only the Bohr model

00:33:05.500 --> 00:33:07.030
that could explain it all.

00:33:07.030 --> 00:33:08.410
It was only the Bohr model.

00:33:08.410 --> 00:33:14.800
And the reason is that all the
transitions were out there,

00:33:14.800 --> 00:33:18.760
and you need the complete
picture of these changes

00:33:18.760 --> 00:33:21.250
in energy between these levels.

00:33:21.250 --> 00:33:23.590
You need to know about
that to understand

00:33:23.590 --> 00:33:24.778
all those transitions.

00:33:24.778 --> 00:33:26.320
And at the time, it
was a real thing.

00:33:26.320 --> 00:33:28.278
If you were like, I want
to get something named

00:33:28.278 --> 00:33:30.670
after myself in
science, you'd be like

00:33:30.670 --> 00:33:34.030
let me go and find a new set
of transitions, which are just

00:33:34.030 --> 00:33:35.498
basically lines, right?

00:33:35.498 --> 00:33:37.540
It's that you're not seeing
a continuous spectrum

00:33:37.540 --> 00:33:39.538
in some part of space.

00:33:39.538 --> 00:33:41.080
You're instead seeing
discrete lines.

00:33:41.080 --> 00:33:42.340
And look at this, right?

00:33:42.340 --> 00:33:45.160
So like Lyman--
you could go down

00:33:45.160 --> 00:33:50.350
to n equals 1 like we did there,
2 to 1, so from here to here.

00:33:50.350 --> 00:33:53.580
But you could also
go from 3 down to 1.

00:33:53.580 --> 00:33:56.860
You could also go from
4, and that's a series.

00:33:56.860 --> 00:34:00.123
Then you could also transition
not from to 1 but to 2 or to 3,

00:34:00.123 --> 00:34:01.540
and each one of
those is a series.

00:34:01.540 --> 00:34:04.440
And then I think finally
they're like, OK, no more names.

00:34:04.440 --> 00:34:08.188
After Pfund, we're
done with naming.

00:34:08.188 --> 00:34:09.730
But all those series
are still there.

00:34:09.730 --> 00:34:12.280
Those are the
allowed transitions.

00:34:12.280 --> 00:34:16.929
So you can also imagine
that it's not just emission.

00:34:16.929 --> 00:34:18.550
So we've been talking
about emission.

00:34:18.550 --> 00:34:22.360
You get these lines when an
electron goes down in energy.

00:34:22.360 --> 00:34:24.639
Remember, like the
ball, the mgh goes down.

00:34:24.639 --> 00:34:29.409
It gives off photons in
these quantized energies,

00:34:29.409 --> 00:34:30.880
but it can be the opposite.

00:34:30.880 --> 00:34:32.050
You could shine light.

00:34:32.050 --> 00:34:35.020
So I could get a continuous
spectrum here and shine it

00:34:35.020 --> 00:34:40.230
on an atom, and that atom
absorbs light literally

00:34:40.230 --> 00:34:42.679
by exciting electrons.

00:34:42.679 --> 00:34:45.960
An electron is in some state,
maybe the ground state,

00:34:45.960 --> 00:34:50.040
and some energy comes along
that exactly takes it up

00:34:50.040 --> 00:34:53.190
to one of these states.

00:34:53.190 --> 00:34:58.440
But it can't sort of
take you up there.

00:34:58.440 --> 00:35:00.330
It's got to take you there--

00:35:00.330 --> 00:35:01.200
quantization.

00:35:01.200 --> 00:35:04.770
That's why you see
these lines here.

00:35:04.770 --> 00:35:09.890
These are the places where
that atom can absorb a photon

00:35:09.890 --> 00:35:12.620
because it exactly
corresponds to a transition

00:35:12.620 --> 00:35:16.100
from one discrete
level to another.

00:35:16.100 --> 00:35:18.950
So it works both ways,
absorbing photon energy,

00:35:18.950 --> 00:35:20.510
promoting electrons up.

00:35:23.260 --> 00:35:26.670
And when we do X-rays, we
just ionize everything.

00:35:26.670 --> 00:35:29.340
But right now, we're
staying within the potential

00:35:29.340 --> 00:35:30.340
of the atom.

00:35:30.340 --> 00:35:35.850
So we're staying
within these levels,

00:35:35.850 --> 00:35:37.770
absorbing or emitting energy.

00:35:37.770 --> 00:35:39.408
So I could answer
questions like this.

00:35:39.408 --> 00:35:40.950
If you look at a
question like this--

00:35:40.950 --> 00:35:41.610
I forget.

00:35:41.610 --> 00:35:45.180
I think this might be from
a couple of years ago quiz,

00:35:45.180 --> 00:35:47.760
so you might get something
like this tomorrow.

00:35:47.760 --> 00:35:48.870
No, you won't.

00:35:48.870 --> 00:35:50.760
I just looked at a
few faces, and no

00:35:50.760 --> 00:35:52.900
because we're talking
about it today.

00:35:52.900 --> 00:35:55.350
So you wouldn't get it
tomorrow because, remember,

00:35:55.350 --> 00:35:59.300
you only get quizzed on things
that we did ending on Monday.

00:35:59.300 --> 00:36:00.660
It wouldn't be fair otherwise.

00:36:00.660 --> 00:36:04.050
But next week, OK.

00:36:04.050 --> 00:36:06.080
What's the lowest-energy
photon shown?

00:36:10.220 --> 00:36:11.210
Which one is it?

00:36:11.210 --> 00:36:12.200
These are photons now.

00:36:12.200 --> 00:36:13.408
We think of these as photons.

00:36:15.660 --> 00:36:16.620
Red.

00:36:16.620 --> 00:36:21.390
High wavelength, lower energy.

00:36:21.390 --> 00:36:22.080
OK, good.

00:36:22.080 --> 00:36:24.390
That was over here.

00:36:24.390 --> 00:36:26.310
Now what about what
transition give us

00:36:26.310 --> 00:36:29.640
the line at 639 nanometers?

00:36:29.640 --> 00:36:34.470
Hello, [? Humana. ?]
639 nanometers.

00:36:34.470 --> 00:36:37.740
So I want to know what
electron transition did that.

00:36:37.740 --> 00:36:40.260
Well, to solve that problem--

00:36:40.260 --> 00:36:42.900
to solve that problem, I
need to do some erasing.

00:36:42.900 --> 00:36:49.660
Maybe I'll just draw
it in here because what

00:36:49.660 --> 00:36:58.170
you have is minus
13.6 eV times--

00:36:58.170 --> 00:37:03.000
I'm assuming z equals 1 because
did I say it was hydrogen?

00:37:03.000 --> 00:37:06.430
I did, hydrogen. z equals 1.

00:37:06.430 --> 00:37:08.520
So z goes away, 1 squared.

00:37:08.520 --> 00:37:15.270
So minus 13.6 eV times 1 over
the final state squared minus 1

00:37:15.270 --> 00:37:18.975
over the initial quantum state
squared quantum number squared.

00:37:21.820 --> 00:37:26.760
So that's going to equal
639 nanometers, which we now

00:37:26.760 --> 00:37:30.030
know we can convert, thanks
to Planck and Einstein,

00:37:30.030 --> 00:37:31.770
into an energy.

00:37:31.770 --> 00:37:33.630
We now know that,
a photon energy.

00:37:33.630 --> 00:37:38.970
It's that photon, that unit,
that discrete unit that

00:37:38.970 --> 00:37:45.360
does it, and this is
1.94 eV because it

00:37:45.360 --> 00:37:47.218
comes from 639 nanometers.

00:37:47.218 --> 00:37:48.510
Back and forth, back and forth.

00:37:48.510 --> 00:37:51.870
We've got to get into that
mode and into that mood.

00:37:51.870 --> 00:37:56.230
Back and forth, energy,
frequency, wavelength.

00:37:56.230 --> 00:37:58.200
And so I won't do the
math here, but the answer

00:37:58.200 --> 00:38:04.590
comes into something
like it's the 4 to 3.

00:38:04.590 --> 00:38:12.050
4 to 3 is the transition
that gives you that.

00:38:12.050 --> 00:38:13.630
We're not going to go--

00:38:13.630 --> 00:38:15.110
some day.

00:38:15.110 --> 00:38:16.805
We're not going to
go kind of all--

00:38:16.805 --> 00:38:18.380
this is a fairly
simple one to see.

00:38:18.380 --> 00:38:23.720
We're not going to go like n
equals 528 to n equals 7,000,

00:38:23.720 --> 00:38:25.770
but some simple kind
of back and forth.

00:38:25.770 --> 00:38:29.900
Just get into that mode of
looking at these transitions

00:38:29.900 --> 00:38:32.270
as changes in energy,
which means the emission

00:38:32.270 --> 00:38:33.980
or absorption of a photon.

00:38:33.980 --> 00:38:36.480
That's what I want you
to get out of this.

00:38:36.480 --> 00:38:38.780
And you can go further than
that and answer questions

00:38:38.780 --> 00:38:41.300
like power questions.

00:38:41.300 --> 00:38:42.800
So here's a question.

00:38:42.800 --> 00:38:44.780
If your favorite radio
station broadcasts

00:38:44.780 --> 00:38:47.450
at a frequency of 107.9--

00:38:47.450 --> 00:38:51.470
I'm not saying that's mine,
but you got to groove out--

00:38:51.470 --> 00:38:53.870
and a power output
of 50 kilowatts,

00:38:53.870 --> 00:38:58.732
how many photons are emitted
by the transmitter each second?

00:38:58.732 --> 00:39:01.190
I just want to give you this
as another example of thinking

00:39:01.190 --> 00:39:01.910
about this.

00:39:01.910 --> 00:39:07.120
Light, energy-- light to
energy, photons, particles.

00:39:07.120 --> 00:39:16.400
And here, you can get that by
simply knowing what power is.

00:39:16.400 --> 00:39:20.690
And if you know what power
is, it's watts per second.

00:39:20.690 --> 00:39:23.310
Sorry, watts is
joules per second.

00:39:23.310 --> 00:39:26.250
And so the energy here is h nu.

00:39:26.250 --> 00:39:27.750
And I won't go
through all the math,

00:39:27.750 --> 00:39:29.540
but I'll leave the answer here.

00:39:29.540 --> 00:39:38.711
You get 7.12 times 10 to the
minus 26 joules per photon.

00:39:38.711 --> 00:39:40.040
Now, how did I get that?

00:39:40.040 --> 00:39:43.550
I got that because I
know the frequency.

00:39:43.550 --> 00:39:44.810
Why am I using joules?

00:39:44.810 --> 00:39:47.600
Remember, I put them both
up there-- joules, eV.

00:39:47.600 --> 00:39:53.840
Ah, because I know my power,
which is 50 kilowatts, which

00:39:53.840 --> 00:39:59.620
is 50,000 joules per second.

00:39:59.620 --> 00:40:01.210
And so I've got
joules in the power.

00:40:01.210 --> 00:40:03.070
I just decided to
stick with joules.

00:40:03.070 --> 00:40:05.350
Joules, eV, joules,
eV, back and forth.

00:40:05.350 --> 00:40:08.200
They're just units.

00:40:08.200 --> 00:40:10.420
If you're not familiar with
changing back and forth,

00:40:10.420 --> 00:40:12.430
you should do a little practice.

00:40:12.430 --> 00:40:16.090
And this gives you
something like 7 times 10

00:40:16.090 --> 00:40:23.150
to the 29th photons per second.

00:40:23.150 --> 00:40:26.720
So you can think about power,
photons, light, energy.

00:40:26.720 --> 00:40:29.000
If I shine that many--

00:40:29.000 --> 00:40:34.470
if I shine that many
on Einstein's medal,

00:40:34.470 --> 00:40:36.270
will the electrons come off?

00:40:36.270 --> 00:40:38.570
That seems like a lot.

00:40:38.570 --> 00:40:41.300
We don't know because we need
to know how much energy it takes

00:40:41.300 --> 00:40:42.717
to liberate the
electron, and then

00:40:42.717 --> 00:40:46.660
that ties into the frequency.

00:40:46.660 --> 00:40:48.490
Radio waves are
much lower energy,

00:40:48.490 --> 00:40:50.920
much, much lower energy.

00:40:50.920 --> 00:40:52.720
Now, this gets me
to why this matters,

00:40:52.720 --> 00:40:54.685
and that's how I want
to end today's lecture

00:40:54.685 --> 00:41:01.060
in the last five minutes because
we talked about emission,

00:41:01.060 --> 00:41:05.410
electrons transitioning
from their discrete orbitals

00:41:05.410 --> 00:41:07.990
as a way of emitting
or absorbing.

00:41:07.990 --> 00:41:10.150
And so you if you
have different atoms,

00:41:10.150 --> 00:41:13.540
there's hydrogen. These
are the absorption lines--

00:41:13.540 --> 00:41:15.370
absorption lines.

00:41:15.370 --> 00:41:16.900
Carbon has absorption lines.

00:41:16.900 --> 00:41:19.330
Oxygen, nitrogen,
sulfur, so lots of things

00:41:19.330 --> 00:41:20.620
have absorption lines.

00:41:20.620 --> 00:41:22.010
Why does this matter?

00:41:22.010 --> 00:41:24.320
Well, of course, because
of the refrigerator.

00:41:27.300 --> 00:41:28.170
I love this plot.

00:41:28.170 --> 00:41:31.440
This is a plot of
the refrigerator.

00:41:31.440 --> 00:41:34.500
This is the size
of the refrigerator

00:41:34.500 --> 00:41:35.640
as a function of year.

00:41:35.640 --> 00:41:38.345
The date only goes to 2002,
but it's roughly stable now.

00:41:38.345 --> 00:41:39.720
Does anybody know
what's limiting

00:41:39.720 --> 00:41:41.796
the size of the refrigerator?

00:41:41.796 --> 00:41:45.272
It was going up and up and up
a lot, and then it stopped.

00:41:45.272 --> 00:41:46.730
Does anybody know
what limits that?

00:41:46.730 --> 00:41:49.090
STUDENT: The average
height [INAUDIBLE]..

00:41:49.090 --> 00:41:51.520
PROFESSOR: It's the
width of the doorway.

00:41:51.520 --> 00:41:52.900
It's the width of the doorway.

00:41:52.900 --> 00:41:55.067
That's why we don't-- that's
why people get a second

00:41:55.067 --> 00:41:55.620
refrigerator.

00:41:58.800 --> 00:42:00.850
And there's energy use.

00:42:00.850 --> 00:42:03.010
Energy Star program, big deal.

00:42:03.010 --> 00:42:04.420
Big deal.

00:42:04.420 --> 00:42:06.220
But I'm talking
about refrigerators

00:42:06.220 --> 00:42:07.840
for a different reason.

00:42:07.840 --> 00:42:11.170
I'm talking about refrigerators
because until the 1980s,

00:42:11.170 --> 00:42:15.190
the way we did cooling
was with a chemical called

00:42:15.190 --> 00:42:16.810
chlorofluorocarbon.

00:42:16.810 --> 00:42:18.310
This is a chemistry
class, and we're

00:42:18.310 --> 00:42:19.990
going to be talking about--

00:42:19.990 --> 00:42:21.520
when we talk about
why this matters,

00:42:21.520 --> 00:42:23.440
we connect to the chemistry.

00:42:23.440 --> 00:42:25.270
Well, the chemistry
there was critical.

00:42:25.270 --> 00:42:28.750
There was a molecule there
called chlorofluorocarbon

00:42:28.750 --> 00:42:30.280
or CFC.

00:42:30.280 --> 00:42:33.250
And the thing
about that molecule

00:42:33.250 --> 00:42:40.120
is that its absorption,
the chlorofluorocarbon--

00:42:40.120 --> 00:42:41.860
so let's write it
down here, CCl3F.

00:42:47.500 --> 00:42:52.420
There's a gas phase, so we
put the little lower script g.

00:42:52.420 --> 00:42:57.550
That molecule has
its own absorption.

00:42:57.550 --> 00:43:00.050
It has its own absorption,
and it has its own reaction,

00:43:00.050 --> 00:43:01.610
and so do other things.

00:43:01.610 --> 00:43:04.270
But it has a really
important reaction

00:43:04.270 --> 00:43:09.510
because when it
absorbs UV rays, then

00:43:09.510 --> 00:43:12.790
it doesn't just like then
like electrons get pumped up.

00:43:12.790 --> 00:43:14.920
No, the whole thing reacts.

00:43:14.920 --> 00:43:23.860
And so this thing degrades
into CCl2F plus chlorine gas,

00:43:23.860 --> 00:43:28.330
and that's once it's up maybe
in the atmosphere, which

00:43:28.330 --> 00:43:30.220
it was getting released
all the time because

00:43:30.220 --> 00:43:31.095
of the refrigerators.

00:43:31.095 --> 00:43:35.080
And the thing is that the
chlorine gas would then

00:43:35.080 --> 00:43:43.570
react with ozone, and this
would go to ClO plus O2,

00:43:43.570 --> 00:43:44.782
and here's the problem.

00:43:44.782 --> 00:43:46.240
I mean, we're
already at a problem,

00:43:46.240 --> 00:43:48.035
but now it's a big
problem because--

00:43:48.035 --> 00:43:48.910
you can already tell.

00:43:48.910 --> 00:43:52.210
But I've got ClO, and
that's really reactive.

00:43:52.210 --> 00:43:56.010
So what happens is
ClO, which was a gas--

00:43:56.010 --> 00:43:58.630
I'm just being careful
here with my subjects--

00:43:58.630 --> 00:44:01.830
would react with atomic
oxygen to give you

00:44:01.830 --> 00:44:05.920
O2 and the chlorine atom back.

00:44:05.920 --> 00:44:08.020
And here's the thing.

00:44:08.020 --> 00:44:09.520
Now I've got my
chlorine atom again.

00:44:09.520 --> 00:44:11.520
These are very reactive
atoms in the atmosphere.

00:44:11.520 --> 00:44:14.460
They love ozone.

00:44:14.460 --> 00:44:19.540
And so this cycle
for one CFC molecule

00:44:19.540 --> 00:44:22.720
would happen 100,000
times, roughly,

00:44:22.720 --> 00:44:24.440
before that chlorine
kind of goes

00:44:24.440 --> 00:44:25.690
away and does something else--

00:44:25.690 --> 00:44:28.905
100,000 ozone molecules per CFC.

00:44:28.905 --> 00:44:29.780
Why does that matter?

00:44:29.780 --> 00:44:31.600
Well, it matters because--

00:44:31.600 --> 00:44:33.520
I love this plot.

00:44:33.520 --> 00:44:39.200
This is the energy of light
from the sun hitting our planet.

00:44:39.200 --> 00:44:44.280
So this is the energy that
you get, and look at this.

00:44:44.280 --> 00:44:46.540
This is the sun at the
top of the atmosphere.

00:44:46.540 --> 00:44:48.337
Here it is on Earth, the red.

00:44:48.337 --> 00:44:49.420
Look at these things here.

00:44:53.090 --> 00:44:55.280
If you could only
see in this spectrum,

00:44:55.280 --> 00:44:58.880
the world would be dark because
there's no light on the planet.

00:44:58.880 --> 00:44:59.380
Why?

00:44:59.380 --> 00:45:02.120
Because it's all absorbed in
the upper atmosphere by water.

00:45:02.120 --> 00:45:06.900
Absorption-- it's the
same principle, Bohr.

00:45:06.900 --> 00:45:09.720
Bohr's idea of electrons
getting promoted

00:45:09.720 --> 00:45:12.660
when they absorb
light is happening,

00:45:12.660 --> 00:45:16.530
and it's protecting us because
right here, see, that's the UV.

00:45:16.530 --> 00:45:19.140
Low wavelength, high energy.

00:45:19.140 --> 00:45:20.640
UV, O3.

00:45:20.640 --> 00:45:21.442
Look at that.

00:45:21.442 --> 00:45:22.650
Look at what it's taking out.

00:45:22.650 --> 00:45:26.820
That little sliver of
yellow is critical.

00:45:26.820 --> 00:45:31.380
And ozone was doing the
job, and it still is.

00:45:31.380 --> 00:45:34.410
But if we tear all that
ozone out with these CFCs,

00:45:34.410 --> 00:45:35.860
we have a crisis.

00:45:35.860 --> 00:45:40.500
And that led to what is one of
the greatest policy decisions

00:45:40.500 --> 00:45:44.220
which was made in 1987-- it's
called the Montreal Protocol--

00:45:44.220 --> 00:45:47.850
to essentially globally
ban-- the US led this.

00:45:47.850 --> 00:45:51.420
This is a great example of
how policy is so critical.

00:45:51.420 --> 00:45:54.300
The US led this
effort to ban CFCs,

00:45:54.300 --> 00:45:57.780
and we're almost back
to normal ozone levels,

00:45:57.780 --> 00:45:58.610
and we avoid this.

00:45:58.610 --> 00:46:01.770
And by the way, if
this had happened,

00:46:01.770 --> 00:46:04.350
the prediction at the time
was roughly 280 million

00:46:04.350 --> 00:46:09.010
additional cases of skin
cancer over this generation.

00:46:09.010 --> 00:46:11.130
This is a very big
deal, and it all

00:46:11.130 --> 00:46:16.740
comes back to
absorption of electrons

00:46:16.740 --> 00:46:18.480
by atoms and molecules.

00:46:18.480 --> 00:46:20.540
See you guys on Friday.

